   "html_text": "Thomas Nagel (2023) has recently defended a more nuanced realist account, recognizing the positive role of subjective experience in advancing moral progress. Contrary to Singer, Nagel does not think that moral progress consists in the gradual recognition and application of a single, timeless, normative principle such as impartial benevolence. Indeed, he thinks this would be a moral loss. Nagel identifies two models of moral progress: one involving the recognition of objective and timeless reason",
   "md_text": "Thomas Nagel (2023) has recently defended a more nuanced realist account, recognizing the positive role of subjective experience in advancing moral progress. Contrary to Singer, Nagel does not think that moral progress consists in the gradual recognition and application of a single, timeless, normative principle such as impartial benevolence. Indeed, he thinks this would be a moral loss. Nagel identifies two models of moral progress: one involving the recognition of objective and timeless reason"
